\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{The half-turn in characteristic two}
\date{2026-10-17}

\p{The coefficient assumption in the
[two-sector decomposition](fdip-000L) is essential. Let #{k} now
be a field of characteristic two. Use the same #{C_2}, group-basis
element #{g}, coordinate modules #{V_k,W_k}, and maps #{P,J}.
The faithful algebra actions still exist, but the algebra is
##{k[C_2]\cong k[z]/(z^2),}
and it has no idempotents other than #{0,1}. Thus it cannot be
isomorphic to #{k\times k}.}

\p{The \vocabk{Group Algebra}{fgap-001H} still has its group
basis, as in \citet{Sections 3.1-3.2, pp. 39-41}{sengupta2010representations}.
The character projectors in
\citet{Section 4.5, p. 63}{sengupta2010representations}
require the group order to be invertible in the field. The following
calculation identifies what replaces that product when the order
is zero in #{k}.}

\proof{
\p{Put #{\epsilon=g-1}. Its coefficient at #{g} is #{1}, so
#{\epsilon\ne0}; nevertheless
##{\epsilon^2=g^2-2g+1=0.}
Since #{g=1+\epsilon}, the pair #{1,\epsilon} is a basis:
##{c+d\epsilon=(c-d)+dg,}
so coefficient comparison proves independence as well as spanning.
Evaluation at #{\epsilon} therefore induces a unital
#{k}-algebra map
##{\begin{aligned}
\Psi:k[z]/(z^2)&\longrightarrow k[C_2],\\
[f(z)]&\longmapsto f(\epsilon).
\end{aligned}}
It is well defined because #{\epsilon^2=0}. Every polynomial has
a unique remainder #{c+dz} modulo the monic polynomial #{z^2}:
write #{f=c+dz+z^2q}, and observe that a multiple of #{z^2}
has zero constant and linear coefficients. Hence the quotient has
basis #{[1],[z]}. The map sends it to the basis #{1,\epsilon},
so it is bijective. The algebra #{k[z]/(z^2)} is the algebra of
\newvocab{dual numbers} over #{k}; its nonzero element #{[z]}
has square zero.}

\p{If #{c+d\epsilon} is idempotent, commutativity and
characteristic two give
##{(c+d\epsilon)^2=c^2.}
Comparison in the basis #{1,\epsilon} forces #{d=0} and
#{c^2=c}. Since #{k} is a field, #{c(c-1)=0} implies
#{c=0} or #{c=1}. Both are idempotent. In contrast,
#{(1,0)} is a nonzero idempotent different from the unit in
#{k\times k}. An algebra isomorphism preserves these properties,
which rules out the product. The field hypothesis matters: a
general characteristic-two ring can already have nontrivial scalar
idempotents.}
}

\p{On the vertex module, the same half-turn still satisfies
#{P^2=I}, while #{-1=1}. Its two proposed eigenspaces now
coincide:
##{\begin{aligned}
\ker(P-I)&=\ker(P+I)\\
 &=\{(x,y,x,y):x,y\in k\}.
\end{aligned}}
This is a two-dimensional subspace of #{k^4}; adding it to itself
does not recover the full module. On the retained module,
##{\begin{aligned}
\ker(J-I)&=\ker(J+I)\\
 &=\{(x,x):x\in k\},
\end{aligned}}
which has dimension one inside #{k^2}. These equalities follow
directly from the coordinate formulas for #{P,J}.}

\p{The nonzero nilpotent is visible in the faithful action as well.
For #{N=P-I},
##{\begin{aligned}
N^2&=P^2-2P+I=0,\\
N\delta_0&=\delta_2-\delta_0\ne0.
\end{aligned}}
The last vector has coefficient #{1} at coordinate #{2}.
Similarly, #{J-I} is nonzero on #{\delta_0^R} and has square
zero. The expressions #{(I\pm P)/2} and #{(I\pm J)/2} are
undefined because #{2=0}. The action and projection intertwiner
survive, but the complementary projectors and eigenspace direct
sum do not. This is a change of coefficient field for the algebra
and modules; the real [Gaussian law](fdip-000M) is not being
extended to characteristic two.}

\p{TauCeti's
\leanref/tauceti{MonoidAlgebra.isIdempotentElem_cyclicTwo_iff}
proves the idempotent obstruction more generally for a commutative
ring without zero divisors in which #{2} is not a unit. A
characteristic-two field meets these hypotheses. The generator is
identified with #{\operatorname{Multiplicative.ofAdd}
(1:\mathbb Z/2\mathbb Z)} as in \ref{fdip-000L}.
The dual-number presentation and the coordinate nilpotent above
are separate explicit calculations.}
